#X obj 60 40 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty tx81zn.i~\ and\ tx81zn~ 24 6 0 22 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 60 76 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Yamaha\ TX81Z\ sound\ engine\ rebuilt\ natively 24 6 0 16 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 60 120 tx81zn.i~ 1;
#X text 190 120 <- click it: the editor, f 64;
#X obj 60 160 dac~;
#X obj 60 206 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty tx81zn.i~ 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 234 tx81zn.i~ is the TX81Z in one object: tx81zn~ (the voice routines of the firmware rebuilt natively) \, the FM editor txfm \, the controller layer txfmcc \, the Launchkey pads and display \, MIDI in and out \, and txedit with every single voice parameter. Put it in a patch \, connect the two left outlets to dac~ and play. Click it to open the FM editor \, see Editors. \; Argument 1 is the MIDI channel (0 or none = 1) \, argument 2 the channel of the pads (0 or none = 10 \, the Drums Channel). Several tx81zn.i~ do not get in each other's way \, each has its own editors. \; It needs the firmware tx81z-v1.6.bin beside tx81zn~ (tables and preset banks A to D are read from it \, inside tx81zn.i~ the patch that holds tx81zn~ is tx81zn.i~.pd itself) \, see ROM and battery RAM. \; tx81zn~ is the bare engine inside \, for patches of your own: raw MIDI bytes from midiin go into its inlet \, its outlet 3 reports every change. Open tx81zn.i~ to see the wiring., f 64;
#X obj 60 520 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Editors 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 548 The FM editor txfm is the window of tx81zn.i~: the rows of the FM view (Controls) \, row k = page k of txfmcc. On top: voice name \, bank I A B C D and patch \, the algorithm with its roles \, random voices. \; txedit (pd txedit below the editor \, click it): all 100 voice parameters in rows of 8 (Voice parameters) \, voice name \, program change \, MIDI channel \, the user bank. Both work on the same bus and stay in sync. \; Each cell: label \, a slider and a number box with the value as from a pot (0 to 127) \, the box beside it the value as on the TX81Z (- when the role has no operator in this algorithm)., f 64;
#X obj 60 738 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Launchkey 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 766 Launchkey: the Novation Launchkey Mini MK4 with the custom firmware maltepeter2000/launchkey_firmware \, set up the same for every instrument of bela-workstation (launchkey/README.md there). \; txfmcc 1: the controller layer. Encoders in mode Custom 1 send CC pages from 20 in rows of 8 on the keys channel \, every change made elsewhere goes back to them on the send channel (txchannel of tx81zn~) \, CC 3 asks for all values. \; txpads 10: the pads in Drum mode (notes 36-51 on channel 10 \, the Drums Channel): top row 36 factory + \, 37 user + \, 38 init (press twice within a second) \, 39-43 own \, bottom row 44 factory - \, 45 user - \, 46 save \, 47-51 own. Own pads of tx81zn~: 39-43 random pluck \, bass \, lead \, brass \, pad \, 47 random strings \, 48 random total. \; Storing asks first: save once shows Store to? and the user slot played or stored last (savearm) \, user + / - choose the slot (saveslot 1 / -1 \, nothing is recalled) \, save again stores there and writes the user bank (save). Any other pad or 10 s cancel (savecancel). Own pads and init after its first press show what they do on the display. \; Pad colours \, sent by txpads itself with noteout on channel 10 (velocity = colour + 32 x mode): factory blue \, user and save green (the user bank) \, init orange (flashing while the second press is awaited) \, save flashing while it asks and white for a moment when stored \, own pads purple \, free pads off. They go out at load \, on leds \, and when the Launchkey asks for them (CC 3 = 127 on channel 10 \, sent when Drum mode is selected). \; lkdisplay: the display lines (display <title> <value>: the control you turn \, Preset \, User \, Init or Stored with the patch name) as SysEx for the Launchkey's display -> midiout., f 64;
#X obj 60 1260 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Inlet 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1288 1 note \, cc \, pgm \, bend messages \, raw MIDI (float \, list \, \;   midi <bytes>) and the messages below., f 64;
#X obj 60 1334 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Outlets 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1362 1 signal: left output (~55.9 kHz resampled). \; 2 signal: right output. \; 3 anything: info \, fm \, voice \, voicedata \, channel \, status \, \;   display <title> <value> \, lcd (splash \, once)., f 64;
#X obj 60 1440 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Presets\ and\ user\ bank 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1468 bank <I|A|B|C|D> <1-32> selects any voice \, bank D included. Program change 0-127 selects I01-I32 and A01-C32 as on the TX81Z. Bank I comes from the battery RAM file \, without it bank I is empty. \; The user bank (64 voices \, two TX81Z voice bulk dumps) is txuser-bank.syx in the folder of your patch \, so every project keeps its own. tx81zn.i~ reads it at load if it is there \, otherwise the bank beside tx81zn~ is the starting point. save \, store and userwrite / userread without a file name use this file. Save the patch first \, an unsaved patch has no folder of its own. tx81z.i~ uses the same file., f 64;
#X obj 60 1658 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Messages 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1686 note <p> <v> [ch] \, cc <n> <v> [ch] \, pgm <n> [ch] \, \;   bend <-8192..8191> [ch] \; <float> / list \, midi <bytes>: raw MIDI bytes ([midiin]) \, \;   SysEx parameter changes and voice dumps included \; bank <I|A|B|C|D> <1-32>: select a voice \; fm <control> <0-127> \, fminfo <control>: FM view \; <name> <value>: a voice parameter \, TX81Z data value \, \;   e.g. alg 4 \, out1 99 (all names: Voice parameters) \; n <name> <0-127>: the same from a pot / CC position \; ninfo <name>: outlet 3 gets info <name> <0-127> <value> \, \;   every change is reported this way by itself \; vced <p> <d> \, aced <p> <d>: raw parameter change \; dumpreq: report every value again (info \, fm \, channel) \; getvoice: outlet 3 gets voicedata <name> <93 VCED> \;   <23 ACED> \, voicedata ...: load that voice \; random <kind> [seed]: a new voice \, \;   kind: pluck bass lead brass pad strings total \; presetstep [n] \, userstep [n]: ROM preset A01-D32 / user \;   slot n after the one played last (the Launchkey pads) \; init: the TX81Z Init Voice \; user <0-63>: recall a slot of the user bank \; store [0-63] \, save: the voice into a slot (save: the last \;   one) \, writes the user bank (see Presets) \; savearm \, saveslot n \, savecancel: the pads' store with a \;   question: show the slot \, move it \, drop it. save after \;   savearm stores into the slot shown. \; userread [file] \, userwrite [file]: the user bank. A file \;   name without a path is beside tx81zn~. \; channel <0-16>: receive channel \, 0 = omni \; tune <0-127>: master tune (64 = centre) \; rom <file> \, nvram <file>: load (the nvram is only read) \; factoryinit: all notes off \, voice A01 \; gain <f>: output gain (default 1), f 64;
#X obj 60 2228 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty FM\ view 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2256 The FM view gives the four operators roles from the algorithm: C = the carriers (what you hear) \, A = the modulator of the main carrier OP1 \, B = the other modulators. A control of a role changes all of its operators and shows the value of the first one. In the end everything is an ordinary TX81Z voice parameter: the voice stays a TX81Z voice (txedit shows every single value). \; Algorithms (> = modulates) and roles: 1 4>3>2>1 C:1 A:2 B:3+4 \, 2 (4+3)>2>1 C:1 A:2 B:3+4 \, 3 (4+(3>2))>1 C:1 A:2 B:3+4 \, 4 ((4>3)+2)>1 C:1 A:2 B:3+4 \, 5 (4>3)+(2>1) C:1+3 A:2 B:4 \, 6 4>(3+2+1) C:1+2+3 A:4 B:- \, 7 (4>3)+2+1 C:1+2+3 A:4 B:- \, 8 4+3+2+1 C:1+2+3+4 A:- B:-. \; Ratio shows the real frequency ratio of coarse and fine. Mix: left the main carrier OP1 \, right the other carriers (the louder side keeps its level). Detune spreads all operators around the centre. ENV C is the amplitude \, ENV A and ENV B are the brightness. Changing the algorithm keeps the operator settings: an operator that becomes a carrier sounds with its output level., f 64;
#X obj 60 2574 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty The\ instrument 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2602 The voice \, note \, modulation and controller routines of the TX81Z firmware v1.6 are rebuilt in C++ and drive the YM2414 directly \, with the same register values as the firmware (tested against the firmware running in tx81z~). A YM2414 core derived from ymfm \, bit-identical to it \, renders the sound. About a third to a sixth of the CPU of tx81z~ \, and parameter changes take effect at once (no SysEx round trip). \; Single mode only: no performance mode \, no micro tuning \, no front panel. The display shows the splash screen once (lcd) \, the voice name comes as voice <name>., f 64;
#X obj 60 2776 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty ROM\ and\ battery\ RAM 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2804 Arguments: [tx81zn~ <firmware> <battery RAM file>] \, paths relative to the patch that holds tx81zn~ (for tx81zn.i~: beside tx81zn~ \, it gives tx81z-v1.6.bin tx81z.nvram). The firmware (64 KB \, e.g. tx81z-v1.6.bin from the MAME set tx81z) is not included: tx81zn~ reads its tables and the preset banks A-D from it. The battery RAM file of tx81z~ is optional and only read: bank I \, master tune and receive channel. tx81zn~ never writes it., f 64;
#X obj 60 2946 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Controls 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2974 The FM view in the order of the editor txfm: one heading per row \, the same title as the row there \, page k on the controller is row k. Each line: name (the label in the editor) \, range \, CC number and what it does. Set them with fm <control> <0-127>., f 64;
